WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:03.375
Welcome to CSC-244.

2
00:00:03.625 --> 00:00:08.025
You’ll combine the Kotlin you already know into a small notes app model.

3
00:00:08.275 --> 00:00:13.200
We’ll add, find, update, and remove notes, then trace the complete sequence.

4
00:00:13.450 --> 00:00:17.825
This cumulative example introduces no new Kotlin syntax.

5
00:00:18.825 --> 00:00:24.188
Keeping data rules in one small class makes them easier to test before

6
00:00:24.188 --> 00:00:25.425
building Android screens.

7
00:00:25.675 --> 00:00:30.525
A screen can request an operation without managing the collection itself.

8
00:00:30.775 --> 00:00:34.050
We’ll focus on how familiar pieces work together.

9
00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:38.725
Imagine you’re building the data-handling part of a notes app.

10
00:00:38.975 --> 00:00:42.275
Each note has an integer ID and a text title.

11
00:00:42.525 --> 00:00:46.700
Our sample begins with ID one and the title Lab ideas.

12
00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:52.011
The caller must supply unique IDs; this small store does not check for

13
00:00:52.011 --> 00:00:52.400
duplicates.

14
00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:56.850
The store should find a note or report that it is missing.

15
00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:00.575
Updating or removing a missing ID should do nothing.

16
00:01:00.825 --> 00:01:03.850
Updating keeps the ID while changing the title.

17
00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:09.490
All data lives in memory during this run; there is no database or persistent

18
00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:09.875
storage.

19
00:01:10.977 --> 00:01:13.352
Let’s open demo.kts.

20
00:01:13.602 --> 00:01:18.602
The standard Run Code button will execute the complete script with the

21
00:01:18.602 --> 00:01:19.852
installed Kotlin compiler.

22
00:01:20.104 --> 00:01:24.838
Start with the data class and a store that owns an empty mutable list of

23
00:01:24.838 --> 00:01:25.154
notes.

24
00:01:25.404 --> 00:01:30.301
The note properties use val, so changing a title will mean creating a

25
00:01:30.301 --> 00:01:31.054
replacement note.

26
00:01:41.634 --> 00:01:44.309
Private keeps the list inside NoteStore.

27
00:01:44.559 --> 00:01:49.524
Its val binding stays attached to the same list, while the mutable list

28
00:01:49.524 --> 00:01:51.434
permits adding and removing elements.

29
00:01:51.684 --> 00:01:56.677
The explicit Note element type tells Kotlin what this initially empty list

30
00:01:56.677 --> 00:01:57.509
will contain.

31
00:01:57.761 --> 00:02:00.661
Now provide the two simplest operations.

32
00:02:00.911 --> 00:02:02.911
Add appends a supplied note.

33
00:02:03.161 --> 00:02:08.304
Find searches for the requested ID and returns a nullable Note, because that

34
00:02:08.304 --> 00:02:09.886
ID might not exist.

35
00:02:18.475 --> 00:02:21.525
Inside the search lambda, it is the current note.

36
00:02:21.775 --> 00:02:25.675
Comparing its ID with the parameter selects the first match.

37
00:02:25.925 --> 00:02:27.900
No match produces null.

38
00:02:28.150 --> 00:02:34.361
Add does not enforce unique IDs, so callers must honor that precondition for

39
00:02:34.361 --> 00:02:36.750
ID-based operations to be unambiguous.

40
00:02:37.007 --> 00:02:40.482
Removal can reuse that search instead of repeating it.

41
00:02:40.732 --> 00:02:45.526
Find the note, check the nullable result, and remove the actual object only

42
00:02:45.526 --> 00:02:46.632
when it exists.

43
00:02:55.633 --> 00:02:59.108
The null check makes note safe to use inside the branch.

44
00:02:59.358 --> 00:03:03.958
When the ID is absent, the branch is skipped and the list stays unchanged.

45
00:03:04.208 --> 00:03:08.983
Other records remain because we remove the one note found for this ID.

46
00:03:09.236 --> 00:03:12.436
Updating follows the same find-and-check structure.

47
00:03:12.686 --> 00:03:17.586
For an existing note, remove the old object and append a copy with the

48
00:03:17.586 --> 00:03:18.286
requested title.

49
00:03:18.536 --> 00:03:21.836
Let’s add that operation and close the store class.

50
00:03:36.445 --> 00:03:41.170
Copy creates the replacement while retaining the omitted ID property.

51
00:03:41.420 --> 00:03:44.670
The original note’s val title is not reassigned.

52
00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:49.070
If the ID is missing, neither removal nor addition happens.

53
00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:54.007
Because the replacement is appended, updating can move a note to the end of

54
00:03:54.007 --> 00:03:57.020
the list; this version does not preserve its position.

55
00:03:57.276 --> 00:03:59.251
Work through a separate case.

56
00:03:59.501 --> 00:04:03.276
Add ID seven with Trip plan and ID eight with Shopping.

57
00:04:03.526 --> 00:04:05.826
Update seven to Weekend trip.

58
00:04:06.076 --> 00:04:11.001
Searching seven now returns Weekend trip, while eight still returns Shopping.

59
00:04:11.251 --> 00:04:14.626
Removing seven makes its next search return null.

60
00:04:14.876 --> 00:04:20.251
Updating or removing an absent ID nine leaves the other record unchanged.

61
00:04:20.503 --> 00:04:23.703
Now connect the operations in our actual script.

62
00:04:23.953 --> 00:04:29.672
Create the store, add Lab ideas under ID one, update its title, print a search

63
00:04:29.672 --> 00:04:33.103
result, remove the note, and print another search result.

64
00:04:50.537 --> 00:04:54.837
Both print expressions use the same safe call and Elvis fallback.

65
00:04:55.087 --> 00:04:58.612
If find returns a note, the safe call reads its title.

66
00:04:58.862 --> 00:05:02.337
If find returns null, the expression uses Missing.

67
00:05:02.587 --> 00:05:07.320
In the separate Trip plan case, that would print Weekend trip before removal

68
00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:08.412
and Missing afterward.

69
00:05:08.686 --> 00:05:11.836
Predict the two lines for our actual ID one.

70
00:05:12.086 --> 00:05:16.868
Follow which note exists after each operation, and decide whether each search

71
00:05:16.868 --> 00:05:18.861
returns a note or null.

72
00:05:19.111 --> 00:05:22.111
Pause and trace the sequence before running it.

73
00:05:28.368 --> 00:05:32.268
Run the script and compare the real output with your prediction.

74
00:05:42.778 --> 00:05:47.353
Android lab ideas is the replacement note’s title after the update.

75
00:05:47.603 --> 00:05:52.178
Missing appears after removal because the second search returns null.

76
00:05:52.428 --> 00:05:57.390
The ID stayed one during the update, so both searches targeted the same

77
00:05:57.390 --> 00:05:58.153
logical note.

78
00:05:58.405 --> 00:06:02.988
The store combines data objects, collection operations, nullable results, and

79
00:06:02.988 --> 00:06:06.655
controlled changes behind a small set of functions.

80
00:06:06.905 --> 00:06:09.280
This run checks the main sequence.

81
00:06:09.530 --> 00:06:14.675
It does not by itself prove every edge case, and none of the notes survive as

82
00:06:14.675 --> 00:06:16.605
saved data after the script ends.

83
00:06:16.856 --> 00:06:20.331
Build a reminder store with the same four operations.

84
00:06:20.581 --> 00:06:25.781
Supply unique IDs, test present and missing IDs, and keep a second reminder

85
00:06:25.781 --> 00:06:27.381
while changing the first.

86
00:06:27.631 --> 00:06:32.320
What evidence shows that the other reminder survives, and that a missing

87
00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:35.056
update or removal leaves the store unchanged?
